# Hardware verification invariants

A false positive tells a company its expensive hardware is broken. Prefer a missed detection over a fabricated failure. All logic must be deterministic and unit-testable.

## Standing bar (every session)

This is a scientific measurement tool. All logic must be deterministic and
unit-testable. Never change a locked threshold, a locked prediction, or an
existing numeric result to accommodate new data. Never add a second
implementation of an existing formula. Never write an interpretation or a
results file in the same commit as the code that produced the numbers.
Prefer the standard library. Do not add a dependency without asking first.

## Forbidden unless the user explicitly requests it

- LLM / model API calls
- Heuristic scores or "confidence" numbers without a measured derivation
- Machine-learning models (training, inference, or pretrained weights used as a judge)
- Network requests

## Thresholds

Every numerical threshold must be traceable to measured data already in the repository (dataset, fixture, recorded trace, or documented measurement). Never hardcode a guessed cutoff, magic number, or "typical" industry value.

When a threshold is needed:

1. Point to the file and statistic it came from (e.g. max observed noise floor in `data/...`).
2. Encode that provenance in a named constant, comment, or test that re-derives it from the data.
3. If the data is not in the repo, stop and ask — do not invent a number.

Locked thresholds and locked predictions in `docs/` are frozen. New data may
falsify them; it may not rewrite them.

## Dependencies

- Never add a dependency that is not already in `pyproject.toml` without asking first.
- Prefer the Python standard library and `torch`.
- Avoid new dependencies aggressively, including transitive "helpers".

## Implementation bar

- Pure functions over hidden global state.
- Same inputs → same outputs (no wall-clock, RNG, or network in the decision path).
- Every decision path covered by unit tests with fixtures from real or recorded measurements.
- Do not paper over uncertainty with scores; emit explicit measured quantities and pass/fail against documented thresholds.
- One formula, one implementation. If a residual, GPD, or checksum already exists, call it — do not re-derive a parallel copy.
- Measurement code and interpretation/results docs land in separate commits.
